    "custom_faqs_HYxeAe": {
      "type": "custom-faqs",
      "blocks": {
        "faq_YDrQGC": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What is HRV (heart rate variability) and why does it matter?",
            "content": "<p>HRV measures the variation in time between your heartbeats, and it's one of the clearest available signals of how well your nervous system and recovery systems are functioning. Higher HRV generally reflects a body that's recovering well and handling stress efficiently, while a declining trend usually shows up before you consciously feel run down.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_Mw8TVy": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Can wearables like Whoop, Oura or Garmin actually measure recovery accurately?",
            "content": "<p>Yes, within their scope. HRV and resting heart rate, the two metrics most wearables lean on, are genuinely validated markers of recovery and nervous system load. They're not measuring cellular health directly, but they are measuring some of its clearest downstream effects, which is why a rising HRV trend and settling resting heart rate are worth paying attention to over weeks, not single nights.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_h4WDBr": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What resting heart rate and HRV trends indicate improving recovery?",
            "content": "<p>Broadly, a resting heart rate that trends down and an HRV that trends up over several weeks both point toward a body that's recovering more efficiently. Single-day swings are normal and usually noise. It's the direction of the trend over 60 to 90 days that actually tells you something.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "faq_YDrQGC",
        "faq_Mw8TVy",
